diff --git a/docs/figma/inventory-assets.md b/docs/figma/inventory-assets.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6a16869
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/figma/inventory-assets.md
@@ -0,0 +1,114 @@
+# Inventory panel: image assets
+
+Source: `design/figma/inventory-panel.json`, frame **Inventory / Desktop** (`12:340`) and the
+**Slot** component set (`20:1`).
+
+Most of the panel is built from UI objects: frames, `UICorner`, `UIStroke`, `UIGradient` and text
+labels. The visuals below can't be drawn that way in Roblox, so they are uploaded images. Until
+they are uploaded, every id in
+`src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/UI/InventoryStyle.luau` is the placeholder
+`rbxassetid://0`, which draws nothing. After uploading, paste each id into the matching field.
+
+## Export rules
+
+- Export PNGs with a transparent background.
+- Sizes below are final pixel sizes. "2x" means Figma's 2x export of a frame of half that size.
+  The screen's `UIScale` goes up to 2 on 4K screens, so crisp art is exported at 2x. Soft shadows
+  are exported at 1x, because a blur looks the same scaled up.
+- Shadows, glows and icons are exported as **shape only**, in white or black at 100%. The code
+  sets their color and opacity (`ImageColor3`, `ImageTransparency`) to the Figma values, so the
+  numbers stay in one place and can be adjusted without exporting again.
+- For 9-slice images, keep the canvas size and positions exactly as given. The slice rect in code
+  depends on them.
+
+## Assets
+
+| Field in `InventoryStyle` | What it is (Figma node) | Export size | How it is drawn in Roblox |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `Image.PanelShadow` | Panel drop shadow (`40:88` effect: black 45%, y 12, blur 32) | 176 × 176, 1x | 9-slice `ImageLabel` behind the panel |
+| `Image.HexPattern` | **Hex Pattern** group (`40:78`): 41 hexagon outlines | 1440 × 760 (2x of 720 × 380) | Stretched `ImageLabel` filling the panel, `ImageTransparency` 0.94 |
+| `Image.SlotGlow` | Selected slot glow (`20:3` effect: #FFC83C 55%, blur 16, spread 2) | 144 × 144, 1x | 9-slice `ImageLabel` under the selected slot |
+| `Image.CloseIcon` | **Icon/Close** (`40:80`) | 40 × 40 (2x of 20 × 20) | 20 × 20 `ImageLabel`, tinted #F5F6FA |
+| `Image.LockIcon` | **Icon/Lock** (`40:30`, `40:100`) | 48 × 48 (2x of 24 × 24) | 24 × 24 `ImageLabel`, tinted #F5F6FA |
+| `BlockArt.<Name>` × 10 | **Block Art** image fill of each slot, with its drop shadow | 160 × 160 (2x of 80 × 80) | 80 × 80 `ImageLabel` in the slot, `ScaleType.Fit` |
+
+### PanelShadow
+
+Roblox has no blurred shadow that works in live games yet. `UIShadow` is in the API but still in
+beta, so this is a 9-slice image.
+
+- Make a 176 × 176 frame with no fill. Inside it, place a 96 × 96 rectangle with corner radius 16
+  at x 40, y 28, filled black.
+- Give the rectangle a drop shadow: black **100%**, x 0, y 12, blur 32, spread 0. Turn off "Show
+  behind transparent areas", then set the rectangle's fill opacity to 0%. The export is the shadow
+  ring only, with a hole where the panel sits. The panel is 8% see-through, and Figma doesn't draw
+  the shadow behind it either.
+- In code: `SliceCenter = Rect.new(56, 44, 120, 108)`, drawn 40 px past the panel on the left and
+  right, 28 px above and 52 px below. Color is black, `ImageTransparency` 0.55.
+
+### HexPattern
+
+This is the hexagon outline grid at 6% opacity, clipped to the panel. A `ClipsDescendants`
+frame clips to a plain rectangle and ignores `UICorner`, so the clip is baked into the image.
+
+- Duplicate the Panel frame (`40:88`) at 720 × 380 and hide everything except **Hex Pattern**.
+  Set the group's opacity to 100%, remove the panel fill, stroke and effects, and keep "Clip
+  content" and the 16 px corner radius.
+- Export the frame at 2x: white 2 px hexagon strokes on a transparent background, clipped to the
+  rounded rect.
+- In code: fills the panel, `ImageTransparency` 0.94 (the group's 6% opacity). It sits under the
+  2 px border.
+- The panel is 380 tall because ten block types fill two rows of six. If a block type is added
+  and the panel grows a row, export this again at the new height.
+
+### SlotGlow
+
+- Make a 144 × 144 frame with no fill. Inside it, place a 96 × 96 rectangle with corner radius 12
+  at x 24, y 24.
+- Give the rectangle a drop shadow: white **100%**, x 0, y 0, blur 16, spread 2. Set its fill
+  opacity to 0% with "Show behind transparent areas" off, as for the panel shadow.
+- In code: `SliceCenter = Rect.new(36, 36, 108, 108)`, drawn at 144 × 144 and centered on the slot.
+  Tinted #FFC83C, `ImageTransparency` 0.45. Shown only on the selected slot. Neighboring slots
+  draw over its edges, as they do in Figma.
+
+### CloseIcon and LockIcon
+
+Export each vector at its full bounds (20 × 20 and 24 × 24) at 2x, in solid white, which gives
+40 × 40 and 48 × 48. The code tints them with `ImageColor3`.
+
+### BlockArt (Brick, Dirt, Grass, Window, Wood, Stone, Sand, Ice, Lamp, Gold)
+
+These are bitmaps in Figma (`IMAGE` fills, `scaleMode: FIT`). Each one has a drop shadow (black
+40%, y 4, blur 8) that Roblox can't draw, so the shadow is baked into the image.
+
+- For each block type, make an 80 × 80 frame with no fill. Place the 56 × 56 **Block Art**
+  rectangle at x 12, y 12, keep its drop shadow as designed, and export the frame at 2x.
+- In code: an 80 × 80 `ImageLabel` at slot offset (8, 4), which puts the 56 × 56 art at (20, 16)
+  as in Figma. Locked slots set `ImageTransparency` to 0.6, matching the Content frame's 40%
+  opacity, which dims the baked shadow too.
+- Each of the ten slot instances in the screen has its own image (`40:0`, `40:4`, … `40:38`).
+  Export from those, not from the component set, which shows Brick in every variant. Name files
+  after the `BlockTypes` names (`Brick.png` … `Gold.png`).
+
+## Built from UI objects, not images
+
+These are listed so nobody exports them:
+
+- Panel fill (#171A26, 92%), 16 px corners, 2 px inside border (#3A4060): `Frame`, `UICorner`,
+  and `UIStroke` with `BorderStrokePosition.Inner`.
+- Slot gradients and borders, including the 3 px outside gold border when selected:
+  `UIGradient` (rotation 90) and `UIStroke`.
+- Equip button gradient and its 3 px inner bottom shadow: a darker copy of the gradient behind a
+  face that is 3 px shorter. No image needed.
+- Close button circle: `UICorner` at 50%.
+- Text shadows on the title (y 2, black 50%, no blur) and counts (y 1, black 60%, blur 2): a black
+  copy of the label offset down. The title's shadow matches Figma exactly; the counts lose the
+  2 px blur.
+
+## Not reproduced
+
+- **Background blur** (panel `BACKGROUND_BLUR` 24): Roblox can't blur only what's behind a GUI
+  element. `Lighting.BlurEffect` blurs the whole 3D view instead. With the panel 92% opaque,
+  the difference is small.
+- **Title letter spacing** (+2): `TextLabel` has no tracking setting, so the title is a little
+  narrower than in Figma.
